            "turn_number": 0,
            "source": "document",
            "content": "# Change-control checklist — platform facilities\n\nApplies to every change raised against the facility estate, including work carried out by vendors under\na framework agreement. The checklist is filled in by the change raiser and countersigned by the shift\nsupervisor before the ticket leaves draft.\n\n## Before the change\n\n- [ ] A change record exists with a single named owner, a start and end window, and a rollback step written\n      by someone other than the person who will execute it.\n- [ ] The rollback step names the exact state to restore and the person who verifies it afterwards.\n- [ ] Any safety interlock that must be bypassed is listed, together with the mitigation that replaces it\n      while the bypass is active.\n- [ ] The affected room and rack are named. \"Facilities\" is not an acceptable location.\n- [ ] A second engineer has read the plan and signed it. For changes that touch power distribution the\n      reviewer must be from the electrical team.\n\n## During the change",
            "score": 0.46930086555271017,
            "rank": 1,
            "char_count": 991,
            "estimated_tokens": 247,
            "metadata": {
              "title": "Change-control checklist",
              "version": "2026.01",
              "authority": "policy",
              "published": "2026-01-09",
              "doc_id": "bg_01",
              "source_path": "C:\\Users\\sgraz\\Documents\\Stefano\\UNS\\Tesis\\agent-memory-benchmark\\datasets\\documents\\calibration\\bg_01.md",
              "sha256": "8a24fae2768abdc29810d45dc4faab1f4de47684bdb16049c7845d306900f16a",
              "parent_chunk_id": "doc_bg_01",
              "chunk_index": 0,
              "chunk_count": 3
            }
          },
          {
            "chunk_id": "doc_bg_04_c1",
            "session_id": "corpus",
            "turn_number": 0,
            "source": "document",
            "content": "## Structure of a note\n\nThe first paragraph says what customers can observe, in their terms. The second says what is being done and\nby whom. The third, when known, says when the next update will follow. Anything else — hypotheses, internal\ntooling names, the names of engineers — is left out of the customer-facing note and recorded in the internal\ntimeline instead.\n\nInternal notes carry more detail. They name the working theory, the person testing it, and the two or three\ndiagnostics already excluded. An internal note that lists every alarm without saying which ones were ruled\nout costs the next responder the same investigation twice.\n\n## Postmortem\n\nA postmortem is written for every S1 and for any S2 that repeated within the quarter. It is blameless in the\nnarrow sense that it names systems and decisions rather than individuals, and it ends with no more than\nthree actions, each with an owner and a date. Postmortems with ten actions are treated as having none: the\nreview samples whether the actions were completed, and a list nobody finishes scores worse than a short one\nthat closes.\n\n## Escalation touchpoints",
            "score": 0.41134805918460204,
            "rank": 2,
            "char_count": 1125,
            "estimated_tokens": 281,
            "metadata": {
              "title": "Incident communications template",
              "version": "2025.08",
              "authority": "template",
              "published": "2025-08-19",
              "doc_id": "bg_04",
              "source_path": "C:\\Users\\sgraz\\Documents\\Stefano\\UNS\\Tesis\\agent-memory-benchmark\\datasets\\documents\\calibration\\bg_04.md",
              "sha256": "7bc7f184a045244cfbbb28ed22fc9061c78983fab664eb5bf06f3cd2b958c95f",
              "parent_chunk_id": "doc_bg_04",
              "chunk_index": 1,
              "chunk_count": 3
            }
          },
          {
            "chunk_id": "doc_bg_08_c1",
            "session_id": "corpus",
            "turn_number": 0,
            "source": "document",
            "content": "Handovers are written, not spoken. A spoken handover reaches the next shift and nobody else; a written one\nreaches the engineer who is called at three in the morning two weeks later and needs to know why a circuit\nwas left isolated.\n\n## Standing notes\n\nThe plant rooms are visited on a walking round twice per shift. Round times are recorded, because a round\nthat is consistently recorded at the same minute is a round that is being signed off from the office. The\nsecurity lead samples the timestamps monthly and has, on two occasions, asked for the round to be repeated\nunder observation.\n\nRooms under construction are excluded from the round only while the contractor's permit is open, and the\nexclusion ends automatically when the permit expires. An exclusion that outlives its permit is treated as a\nfinding in the next audit.\n\n## Out of hours",
            "score": 0.4110164994698602,
            "rank": 3,
            "char_count": 848,
            "estimated_tokens": 212,
            "metadata": {
              "title": "Escalation matrix and shift handover",
              "version": "2025.12",
              "authority": "policy",
              "published": "2025-12-11",
              "doc_id": "bg_08",
              "source_path": "C:\\Users\\sgraz\\Documents\\Stefano\\UNS\\Tesis\\agent-memory-benchmark\\datasets\\documents\\calibration\\bg_08.md",
              "sha256": "d2e2ee5a4a4fc4617975ece2456ccf6d3b521256be8a4a2123f1fac32f32511e",
              "parent_chunk_id": "doc_bg_08",
              "chunk_index": 1,
